These are the changes I recommend:
=20
1. Non-directory error =E2=80=94 Page 7, =C2=A75.1 (should fix)
    Insert as a new sentence right after the first paragraph of =C2=A75.1 (=
after line 326, "=E2=80=A6authorization mechanisms."):
New:
Because the attribute applies only to directory objects, a server MUST retu=
rn NFS4ERR_INVAL in response to a GETATTR or SETATTR that requests fattr4_u=
ncacheable_dirent_metadata on an object that is not a directory.
=20
2. Inheritance statement =E2=80=94 Page 7, =C2=A75.1 (minor)
    Insert immediately after the sentence added in #2:
New:
This attribute is set per directory. This document does not define propagat=
ion of the attribute to subdirectories created within a directory on which =
it is set; any such inheritance is a matter of local server policy.
=20
3. Security write-side / DoS note =E2=80=94 Page 12, =C2=A710 (should fix)
    Insert as a new paragraph after the authorization paragraph (after line=
 565, "=E2=80=A6does not define a new authorization model."):
New:
Because the attribute is visible to and affects the caching behavior of all=
 honoring clients, servers should consider the implications of allowing unp=
rivileged users to set or clear it. Setting the attribute on a directory fo=
rces honoring clients to abandon READDIR caching and refetch directory-entr=
y metadata on every enumeration, which can increase load on the server and =
on other clients. A server MAY restrict modification of the attribute based=
 on administrative configuration, export policy, or ownership.
=20
4. Trim repetition =E2=80=94 Page 3, =C2=A71 (editorial)
    The best-effort/change-attribute point appears in the Abstract, =C2=A71=
 para 1 (101=E2=80=93108), =C2=A71 para 3 (117=E2=80=93126), =C2=A72 (151=
=E2=80=93161), and =C2=A73 (177=E2=80=93193).        Simplest fix: replace =
=C2=A71 paragraph 3 (lines 117=E2=80=93126), which is the most redundant, k=
eeping only its unique cross-reference.
=20
Replace (lines 117=E2=80=93126):
"Client implementations may cache the file attributes returned by READDIR a=
longside the dirents themselves. This caching is inherently best-effort: th=
e file attributes can change at any time as a result of writes to the under=
lying files, which the directory's change attribute does not track. When th=
is caching returns stale size and timestamp information for files that have=
 been modified concurrently, it also undermines the effectiveness of uncach=
eable file data semantics ([I-D.ietf-nfsv4-uncacheable-files]) in the same =
deployment. This can lead to applications observing inconsistent metadata a=
nd data views even when file data caching is disabled."
=20
With:
"When this best-effort caching returns stale size and timestamp information=
 for concurrently modified files, it also undermines the effectiveness of u=
ncacheable file data semantics ([I-D.ietf-nfsv4-uncacheable-files]) in the =
same deployment: applications can observe inconsistent metadata and data vi=
ews even when file data caching is disabled."
=20
I hope this helps. I used Claude for the review.
=20
Sorin Faibish
